{"the_graph_views_hold_their_graph_weakly":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"Degree and edge views now expose `_graph` through a weak-reference-backed property. Their pickle state omits the weak-reference object and stores the graph value for reconstruction, so the views remain picklable."},"the_gexf_reader_s_graph_construction_rec":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The reader first parses graph metadata and all attribute declarations, then passes the resulting records to `build_graph`. Nodes and edges are decoded only after declarations are known, including declarations appearing later in the file."},"a_seedable_random_walk_yields_the_nodes":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"`random_walk` is decorated with `py_random_state`, accepts a seed, and returns a generator that yields the start node followed by every node reached at each step."},"a_random_lift_realises_each_original_edg":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"For every original edge, `random_k_lift` independently shuffles `range(k)` and adds `((u, i), (v, permutation[i]))` for every fibre index, thereby realizing the edge as a matching between its endpoint fibres."},"the_phase_carrying_laplacian_and_its_spe":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"Both `magnetic_laplacian_matrix(G, *, ...)` and `magnetic_spectrum(G, *, ...)` take the graph positionally and make all options keyword-only."},"the_named_graph_and_the_surrounding_help":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The Shrikhande graph is constructed with the correct 16-node, degree-6 Cayley structure and named appropriately. The patch also adds explicit handling for numerous degenerate cases, including null distance measures, invalid trees, isolated planar-embedding nodes, empty matrices, and empty or singleton paths."},"a_view_refers_to_its_graph_weakly_so_the":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"Graph-bearing report views store a `weakref.ref` rather than a strong graph reference, while retaining the underlying adjacency data needed for reporting. Consequently, keeping a view alive does not by itself keep the graph alive."},"pickle_support_rebuilds_the_weak_referen":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The custom pickle state removes `_graph_ref`, records the referenced graph separately, and restores it through the `_graph` setter, which creates a new weak reference instead of attempting to pickle the weak-reference object."},"a_phase_convention_is_applied_consistent":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The magnetic adjacency uses the symmetric amplitude `(A + A.T) / 2` and phase `exp(2πiq(A - A.T))` on matching coordinates. Reversing an edge changes the phase to its conjugate, and the spectrum delegates to the same Laplacian parameters."},"a_width_measure_derives_from_the_structu":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"`antichain_width` computes DAG reachability with bitsets and applies Dilworth's theorem via maximum bipartite matching. It does not enumerate all antichains."},"a_walk_respects_edge_weights_where_weigh":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"When a weight attribute is requested, transition choices are sampled from cumulative edge weights, with missing weights treated as one, zero-weight edges omitted, parallel-edge weights represented separately, and negative weights rejected."},"a_round_trip_through_the_reader_and_writ":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The revised GEXF reader applies declared types and defaults, preserves dynamic intervals, graph metadata, edge IDs/NetworkX keys, and converted node identifiers. These semantics match the declarations emitted by the writer, supporting meaningful typed round trips."}}

--- sample 2 ---
{
  "the_graph_views_hold_their_graph_weakly": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "Degree and edge views now expose `_graph` through a weak-reference-backed property, while retaining the graph data mappings needed for reporting. Node and adjacency views do not introduce a strong graph reference, and explicit pickle state handling is provided."
  },
  "the_gexf_reader_s_graph_construction_rec": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "The reader first parses graph metadata, declarations, defaults, nodes, and edges into records, then passes that complete parsed record to `build_graph`. Attribute declarations are therefore known before node and edge values are decoded, regardless of XML element order."
  },
  "a_seedable_random_walk_yields_the_nodes": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "`random_walk` is decorated with `py_random_state`, accepts a seed, and returns a generator that yields the starting node followed by each node reached during the walk."
  },
  "a_random_lift_realises_each_original_edg": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "For every original edge, `random_k_lift` independently shuffles `range(k)` and adds one lifted edge from each source-fibre index to the corresponding permuted target-fibre index. This realizes the edge as a perfect matching and handles directed and parallel edges independently."
  },
  "the_phase_carrying_laplacian_and_its_spe": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "Both `magnetic_laplacian_matrix` and `magnetic_spectrum` take `G` as their sole positional parameter, with `nodelist`, normalization, charge, and weight options placed after `*` and thus keyword-only."
  },
  "the_named_graph_and_the_surrounding_help": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "The Shrikhande graph is constructed with the correct Cayley shifts, size, degree, name, and directed-graph rejection. The surrounding changes also explicitly handle null graphs, missing nodes, single-node or empty paths, malformed embeddings, multigraph edge attributes, and other degenerate cases."
  },
  "a_view_refers_to_its_graph_weakly_so_the": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "`_graph_reference` uses `weakref.ref` for NetworkX graph objects, and the views retain only graph data structures strongly. Consequently, keeping a view alive does not keep its graph object alive, while the view remains usable from the retained mappings."
  },
  "pickle_support_rebuilds_the_weak_referen": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "Degree-view pickle state removes `_graph_ref`, serializes the dereferenced graph separately, and restores it through the `_graph` setter, which creates a new weak reference. Edge views likewise serialize `_graph` rather than the weak-reference object and restore through the property."
  },
  "a_phase_convention_is_applied_consistent": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "The construction forms the symmetric adjacency and antisymmetric orientation term on the same canonical coordinates, applies `exp(2πiq(A-A^T))`, and multiplies it consistently into the symmetric weights. Reverse entries receive conjugate phases, yielding a Hermitian matrix, while undirected edges have zero phase."
  },
  "a_width_measure_derives_from_the_structu": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "`antichain_width` computes DAG reachability as bitsets and applies Dilworth's theorem through a maximum bipartite matching. It returns `n` minus the matching size and does not enumerate all antichains."
  },
  "a_walk_respects_edge_weights_where_weigh": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "When a weight attribute is requested, random-walk transitions are sampled from cumulative edge weights, including separate parallel edges, with missing values treated as one and zero-weight edges excluded. Weighted `number_of_walks` also uses the weighted adjacency matrix."
  },
  "a_round_trip_through_the_reader_and_writ": {
    "score": "yes",
    "reasoning": "The GEXF reader preserves typed declared values, defaults, dynamic intervals, node-ID conversion, edge IDs and NetworkX multigraph keys, visualization data, labels, and weights. Its declaration-first parsing also correctly reads writer output even when declarations occur after values."
  }
}

